  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b2.money-and-economy.living-on-a-budget",
      "level": "B2",
      "topics": ["money-and-economy"],
      "title": "Living on a budget",
      "text": "When prices started to rise last year, Sofía and her husband realized that their salaries were no longer enough to make ends meet. The cost of living in their city had gone up much faster than their pay, and inflation was the main topic of every conversation. So they sat down and wrote their first real budget. They listed everything they spent money on and were surprised by the small things: coffee on the way to work, food delivered to the door and subscriptions they never used. They also changed some habits. Instead of having their car repaired at the dealer, they now take it to a small local garage, which charges half the price. Sofía learned to cut her own hair, although she still has it colored at a salon twice a year. The biggest decision was about their debt. They had borrowed money to buy furniture, and the interest rate on the loan was very high, so they paid it off first. \"We can't afford everything we want,\" Sofía says, \"but for the first time we know where our money goes.\"",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
